---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
import { getSitePage, REVIEWED_DATE, REVIEWED_ISO } from "../data/sitePages";

const page = getSitePage("/corrections/");
---

<BaseLayout
  title={page.title}
  description={page.description}
  alternateMd="/corrections/index.md"
  pageType="WebPage"
  dateModified={REVIEWED_ISO}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Corrections" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>Corrections and updates</h1>
      <p class="small">Last reviewed: {REVIEWED_DATE}</p>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        A technical site changes when a material, standard, carrier rule or page design changes. This page explains
        what we treat as a correction, how quickly we respond, how the change is recorded, and where to disagree
        with our decision.
      </p>

      <h2>What is in scope</h2>
      <p>
        We want reports about factual errors, broken links, references to outdated regulations or standards, and
        wording that is unclear enough to mislead a buyer. A disagreement about style, a request to rank a product
        higher, or a complaint about a quotation is handled through <a href="/contact/">contact</a> rather than the
        correction log. A legal interpretation should come from your adviser; if our page implies that we can give
        one, that is itself an error we need to fix.
      </p>

      <h2>Types of change</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">What it means</th><th scope="col">What appears on the page</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Correction</td><td>A factual statement was wrong: a dimension, standard, process or claim.</td><td>A dated correction note at the foot and an entry in the log below.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Clarification</td><td>The facts were right but the wording could reasonably be misunderstood.</td><td>A dated clarification note when the meaning has changed materially.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Update</td><td>A material, standard, service or page policy changed after publication.</td><td>A revised review date, a note when the change is substantive, and a log entry.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Withdrawal</td><td>A page or claim should no longer be relied on and has been removed from publication.</td><td>A replacement notice or redirect where appropriate, plus a log entry.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Response times</h2>
      <p>
        These are working-day targets for a small team in China Standard Time. A request that needs a supplier,
        carrier or authority to reply may take longer; we will say so in the acknowledgement rather than leave the
        request open without an explanation.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Target</th><th scope="col">What happens</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Acknowledgement of any correction report</td><td>5 business days</td><td>We confirm receipt and whether we have enough information to investigate.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Substantive factual correction</td><td>10 business days</td><td>We verify the point with a source and update or explain why the page stands.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Broken internal or external link</td><td>30 days</td><td>We repair the link, replace it with a stable source or remove the reference.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Correction on a legal or trust page</td><td>5 business days</td><td>We prioritise the affected statement and publish a dated note when changed.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>How a correction is recorded</h2>
      <p>
        A substantive correction gets a short note at the foot of the affected page: the date and a plain
        description of what changed. Examples are “Corrected 21 November 2026: removed an incorrect transit
        estimate” or “Clarified 21 November 2026: the document is requested from the producer, not held by us.”
        The same change is entered in the central log below. The “Last reviewed” date changes whenever the page is
        reviewed, even if no factual statement needed correction.
      </p>

      <h2>Correction log</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Page or area</th><th scope="col">What changed</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>2026-11-21</td><td>Update</td><td>Product visuals and photography policy</td><td>Photographs of printed work were removed; industrial schematics were introduced where a product visual appears.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2026-11-21</td><td>Update</td><td>Trust pages</td><td>Compliance and trust pages were expanded, including new methodology, corrections, disclaimer and accessibility pages.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2026-11-08</td><td>Update</td><td>Product photography</td><td>Product photography was reviewed against the customer-privacy policy; no customer photographs were published.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Substantive claims and silent edits</h2>
      <p>
        We do not make silent edits to substantive claims. Fixing a typo, changing a link label or adjusting page
        formatting does not require a log entry. Changing a dimension, a claim about what a document proves, a
        retention period, a response target or a material recommendation does. If a correction changes a page's
        advice, a reader who cited an earlier version should be able to see that the basis changed.
      </p>

      <h2>Appeal or dissent</h2>
      <p>
        If you think our decision on a correction is wrong, reply to the acknowledgement with the subject
        <strong>Correction appeal</strong> and include any evidence or official source we may have missed. A second
        review is made by someone other than the person who gave the first answer where possible. If the
        disagreement remains unresolved, we will record that the point is disputed rather than pretend the matter
        is settled. We aim to respond to an appeal within 10 business days.
      </p>

      <h2>How to report a correction</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}?subject=Correction`}>{site.email}</a> with the subject
        <strong>Correction</strong>, the page URL, the exact passage, what you believe is wrong and a source we can
        check. For a broken link, send the page and the link target. For privacy, accessibility or an order problem,
        use the route on the relevant policy page so it reaches the right process.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        Related policies: <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> · <a href="/cookies/">Cookies</a> ·
        <a href="/terms/">Terms</a> · <a href="/refund-policy/">Returns</a> ·
        <a href="/shipping-delivery/">Shipping</a> · <a href="/declarations/">Declarations</a> ·
        <a href="/editorial-standards/">Editorial standards</a> · <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> ·
        <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> · <a href="/accessibility/">Accessibility</a> ·
        <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
